Attention deficit hyperactivity disorder
Mental Health & Substance Use
Inattention to details, Hyperactive fidgeting, Impulsivity

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MHS-007
Reason for Visit: Acute behavioral crisis and severe sleep deprivation leading to minor self-injury and total household disruption.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted extreme agitation, inability to remain seated, continuous pacing, and 48 hours of sleep deprivation culminating in a minor impulsive injury (tripping over furniture). Urgent after-hours assessment was necessary to rule out acute substance intoxication, assess the injury, and provide pharmacological/behavioral stabilization prior to routine clinic hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I can't stop moving, my brain won't shut up, and I haven't slept in two days because I have to finish this project."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents in an acute state of exhaustion and hyperarousal. Over the past week, the patient experienced a frequent failure to give close attention to details resulting in careless mistakes on a major assignment. There is chronic difficulty sustaining attention in tasks or play activities, and the patient often does not seem to listen when spoken to directly. Parents report a persistent failure to follow through on instructions and failing to finish schoolwork or duties, alongside severe difficulty organizing tasks and activities. The patient demonstrates avoidance of tasks requiring sustained mental effort until absolute deadlines loom, leading to acute crises. There is frequent loss of items necessary for tasks. Today, the patient is overtly fidgeting with or tapping hands or feet and exhibits a total inability to remain seated in expected situations, leading to an impulsive fall and mild ankle sprain.
Functional Status: Currently overwhelmed, unable to perform basic self-care or regulate sleep.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe caregiver burnout; patient is highly impulsive, creating a risk for further accidental injury.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 | HR: 105 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Exhausted but physically restless, continuous shifting of weight, pressured but coherent speech.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals sinus tachycardia. Musculoskeletal exam of the right ankle shows mild lateral swelling, full range of motion, and able to bear weight (Ottawa ankle rules negative). Neurological exam is grossly intact. Mental state examination highlights prominent inattention to details during questioning, continuous hyperactive fidgeting, and marked cognitive and physical impulsivity.
Environmental Observations: Bedroom is severely disorganized; assignments, clothing, and electronics are scattered, posing significant trip hazards.
Medication Reconciliation: Currently unmedicated for ADHD. No current stimulant use identified.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Attention deficit hyperactivity disorder - Status: Exacerbated (Acute decompensation)
Differentials:

Bipolar I Disorder, Manic Episode - Ruled out/unlikely due to absence of grandiosity, decreased need for sleep (patient wants to sleep but cannot), and lack of psychotic features.

Stimulant Intoxication - Ruled out/unlikely based on collateral history and absence of pupillary dilation, diaphoresis, or severe hypertension.

Generalized Anxiety Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely due to the lifelong chronicity of executive dysfunction preceding the acute anxiety state.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute psychomotor agitation, profound sleep deprivation, and a minor traumatic injury secondary to severe executive dysfunction and impulsivity. Telephone triage correctly identified an escalating household crisis. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically required to clear the ankle injury, definitively rule out acute stimulant toxicity, and prescribe short-term pharmacological sleep aid to interrupt the hyperarousal cycle. Deferring care would have exacerbated the sleep deprivation, increasing the likelihood of a major accidental injury or complete psychological collapse before morning.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Right ankle wrapped with a compression bandage; RICE protocol advised. Prescribed Promethazine 25mg STAT for short-term sedative relief to break the insomnia cycle. Expected investigation results: Conners Comprehensive Behavior Rating Scales showing significant elevations.
Positive Behavioral Change: Implement the "Pomodoro Technique" for tasks. Work for 20 minutes, followed by a mandatory 5-minute physical movement break. Reward completing three cycles with a high-value dopamine activity (e.g., gaming). This structures impulsivity and provides predictable reinforcement.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to community mental health for formal ADHD management and coaching.
DME/Safety (Optional): Compression bandage provided.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48 hours to discuss long-term stimulant pharmacotherapy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient experiences severe chest pain, suicidal ideation, or if the ankle becomes entirely non-weight-bearing.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Acute stabilization provided; long-term management requires specialized assessment and titration of ADHD specific medications.
Clinical Course: Sleep cycle is expected to reset with acute intervention. Underlying executive dysfunction remains chronic and requires structured intervention.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If you cannot bear any weight on your ankle tomorrow, or if you begin having thoughts of self-harm due to frustration, seek emergency medical care immediately.

